[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aab48d1-7883-57e1-aff1-fd79e9f40102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354898} {:answer/id #uuid "72152eb4-0fc3-53d8-a188-8e4769880de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355670} {:answer/id #uuid "92ec785f-426e-5a02-8d3a-69c7a4643e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355959} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dda9f7-91fe-5f6e-959a-cedb2452dc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356576} {:answer/id #uuid "e71e2b51-7eda-5a10-9112-7549c74bde4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71e2b51-7eda-5a10-9112-7549c74bde4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356685} :answer-field/id #uuid "b539eda2-1dcf-5e19-b23e-9491f9a23f90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc51d516-c7eb-513e-b5c1-77d70dc84dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-9084" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a7777-a54c-5144-9dd2-6db2bdfadf58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355029} {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} {:answer/id #uuid "9e198969-77e7-54c7-a4d5-8fcaa9d1d45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356073} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d4dda7-ea67-59f3-a16d-acadacf7c5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356127} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae3ce5-d578-5c1b-a2fb-4ead0dd1fc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} :answer-field/id #uuid "331df704-6bf7-5c71-9966-44b1422cc8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "067b683a-f3df-5f66-b795-e612d7f9ac62" :question/math-academy-id "q-90871" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 1+4{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi {t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8\\pi {t}^{3}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =[2\\pi {t}^{4}-t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2\\pi (1)^{4}-1]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45752412-5eab-5f2e-9849-863afd1a80a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355281} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd2453f-f9a3-5276-a79c-c8be22f1f90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355915} {:answer/id #uuid "9a31a860-83b2-58de-b01d-74f8462a0f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356034} {:answer/id #uuid "df5eb165-913a-565c-9e66-26d94ab94c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356619} {:answer/id #uuid "f50969a8-42bc-5641-9384-149ab59d7d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dd2453f-f9a3-5276-a79c-c8be22f1f90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355915} :answer-field/id #uuid "78435242-7a39-5f27-b4fe-73dc1989d517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "979e3e94-fbe0-52cd-a657-896495d69070" :question/math-academy-id "q-9091" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63104dab-b9d9-53be-8474-ea8fb2a0b8c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355546} {:answer/id #uuid "70d045fd-c63f-590e-9dcd-b6ca75db27c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355661} {:answer/id #uuid "84ec489d-b88f-5fe2-bb9a-2861a977e605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355838} {:answer/id #uuid "cb146048-b6f0-5126-8709-e15a492065fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356436} {:answer/id #uuid "f988899b-4893-5cc9-b1d9-e46edae86ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f988899b-4893-5cc9-b1d9-e46edae86ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356843} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7389917-eab7-58ea-bd1a-97dfc244b293" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7bb92a9-d970-5cb7-a354-b33662cccbde" :question/math-academy-id "q-91035" :question/problem "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}={x}^{2},{f}_{1}=x,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x)+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1)+x(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+x(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =-2+2+x \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073f0cc8-8d22-5f48-a32a-b815cdc30966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354726} {:answer/id #uuid "1558c61e-9f9a-5df6-b0da-983ef71b0b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354855} {:answer/id #uuid "41796d89-233d-56f4-b0f1-f0d1d50545b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $y$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355245} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf51f49-6535-561f-bc34-8d56c15143af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{101}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355765} {:answer/id #uuid "a4cde083-ebe2-56b3-b7cc-99d213f291b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "073f0cc8-8d22-5f48-a32a-b815cdc30966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354726} :answer-field/id #uuid "9918cfc4-0ef1-51b5-91ba-5e92ce40d33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30982409-0430-5b49-824a-6fc7b9e1ff5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-92929" :question/problem "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2x}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{2(0)}+1 \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{0}+1 \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3346b133-465c-5f7c-8f6e-ed203cfa9c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355115} {:answer/id #uuid "6e902916-33fe-5cad-9fa1-d693583d5c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} {:answer/id #uuid "7016e321-e75a-5e1d-9c03-93b9317abb94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355653} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef13b5a-fed1-5020-9287-b841ede6880e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355925} {:answer/id #uuid "e3296fe7-7c8e-558c-bc20-fd9a8725aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e902916-33fe-5cad-9fa1-d693583d5c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355642} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd144ebe-3889-52dd-aeed-40781f7c5ac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e35f675f-b059-5f5e-9a19-8ac6f6c3e6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-92963" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2x-3}+2.$ Given that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/86/8646efab1e707e57bd8ff93b5f3f7b1336ae23588abf2d423a45bbcf702f86ab.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2057b37e-9a25-52a2-8856-8f4beb34ceab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354938} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab81749-85a4-57b9-8f30-e8f4ae82500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355023} {:answer/id #uuid "40c8fdd9-fd96-5afe-ad4d-12d26ac1a384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355240} {:answer/id #uuid "63e2cdfa-588c-5bc3-9788-abfe36e0d301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355553} {:answer/id #uuid "78fa938a-adc2-5782-94ca-e707f0cd21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e2cdfa-588c-5bc3-9788-abfe36e0d301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355553} :answer-field/id #uuid "46219c53-ddb4-5a66-86cb-38dc177aaf35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24488487-89b2-5c0e-9650-62ff06ab7b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93000" :question/problem "Find the root of $f(x)=-15{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1}+10.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n-15{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1}+10=0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by subtracting $10$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1}+10 & =0 \\\\ -15{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1}+\\cancel{10}-\\cancel{10} & =0-10 \\\\ -15{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1} & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $-15,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-15{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1} & =-10 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1} & =\\frac{10}{15} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1} & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $\\frac{2}{3}$ as ${\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{-1}$ to get\n\n$$\n{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{x-1}={\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =-1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f44c7b5-9e57-58b2-aecf-1e41d4eda582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355229} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4c95f3-9b59-525f-bfdf-f63744200e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355230} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaec6d5-2255-5a61-bcea-7ab17d4b1d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355331} {:answer/id #uuid "e322b617-873b-5239-8581-bb1d53e80e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356651} {:answer/id #uuid "f0afc2da-f659-52dc-b243-cb6a4a0f18b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f4c95f3-9b59-525f-bfdf-f63744200e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ff30be3-7e69-5d0b-b60e-3dc0ccdae2fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0000fc83-77f5-5913-a1b9-b3ae53df5b9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9385" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda =10$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-0\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}-10\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357627 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038ad00-cbef-555c-b4ef-4c68141d3844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354658} {:answer/id #uuid "09bfb13a-5032-50df-8a5f-8f4a6c0a4bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{e}^{-2x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354755} {:answer/id #uuid "2f5dcb2c-cea8-56b7-9456-0766f72b8509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355075} {:answer/id #uuid "49966142-bc9a-56bd-a8a1-fa56c7efffc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355318} {:answer/id #uuid "c19a7e5b-8d42-5925-8bf0-cfa378fb7bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{-2x}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038ad00-cbef-555c-b4ef-4c68141d3844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354658} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7a07ed-713b-5880-92ea-6cbd8e8d8cac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3243450-c950-543f-968a-f0bf49df75ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-94025" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={e}^{-\\pi },\\,{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=6{e}^{-\\pi }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5739dd64-2f88-5213-8343-dbcc0aab419e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=(4{x}^{2}-3x+6+\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355442} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5f1918-4c86-5902-ba92-4ee601944ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=({x}^{2}+3x-6-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355604} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce9345f-3bb0-5002-a390-5084ee7a2f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+{x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355624} {:answer/id #uuid "da428518-5570-5425-80d1-4c7e4ee43369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=({x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356582} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3405fb-53c1-55b7-9c99-0e73ee097cc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=(4{x}^{2}-6x+3-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da428518-5570-5425-80d1-4c7e4ee43369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=({x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356582} :answer-field/id #uuid "68009b2f-52c8-5bd7-8d64-94627215630a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e387236f-f1a9-53af-b288-8a51c0d9a784" :question/math-academy-id "q-94078" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y={x}^{4}{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{x}+B{x}^{3}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{4}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-24x+24){e}^{x}+C\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the differential equation to the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we divide both sides of the given equation by ${x}^{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe notice that the forcing term $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}({x}^{3})^{′}-{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right){x}^{3} \\\\  & =3x+x \\\\  & =4x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{4}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-24x+24){e}^{x}, \\\\ v(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={y}_{1}(x)u(x)+{y}_{2}(x)v(x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(-\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}-24x+24){e}^{x})+({x}^{3})(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+{x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3x+6-\\frac{6}{x}){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
